arm64: dts: mediatek: give gemini's i2c6 the vendor's push-pull property

The vendor device tree carries `mediatek,use-push-pull` on
i2c@1100e000 and ours did not. Mainline i2c-mt65xx already honours the
property (it writes I2C_IO_CONFIG_PUSH_PULL to OFFSET_IO_CONFIG at bus
init), and this bus has exactly one slave and one master, so matching the
vendor here costs nothing.

IT DOES NOT FIX WHAT IT WAS TRIED FOR, and that is the useful part of
this commit. It was written to explain why every register on the DA9214
reads back 0x00. Measured on #42, with the property in place, the
symptom is unchanged:

  i2cdetect: 68 69 6a 6b, identically on three consecutive scans
  i2cget 0x68 <reg>:       0x00, or intermittent "Read failed"
  five identical plain reads of 0x6a:  0x0b 0x00 0x00 0x00 0xd6
  i2cset 0x68 PAGE_CON 0x82; read back: 0x00

So the address phase of this bus is perfectly reliable and the data
phase is noise. That is a specific and unusual split: in the address
phase the master drives SDA and the slave only pulls it low to
acknowledge, which works every time; in a read's data phase the slave
drives SDA, and nothing the slave puts there arrives intact.

What that settles: B-43's "Unsupported device id = 0x0", and the
conclusion that there is no DA9214 on this board, are artefacts of a
broken bus. Stock Android has the part bound to its own da9214 driver at
this exact address. The chip is there; we cannot talk to it.

What it does not settle: why. Two candidates, both device-free to
investigate. The pinmux -- we take `i2c6_pins_a` from mt6797.dtsi
(SDA=GPIO152, SCL=GPIO151), while the vendor's board DT sets no pinctrl
on this controller at all, so its pins come from somewhere else and may
not be these. And the clock configuration -- our `clock-div = <10>` is
the DTSI default for every bus, and the vendor's value for this
controller was not captured.

Until one of those is resolved, the DA9214's bucks cannot be identified,
and the CPU-regulator work that cpufreq needs stays blocked. That is
worth more than it sounds: the A53s are running at 897 and 1274 MHz
against maxima of 1547 and 2002 (B-40), so this bus is standing between
the machine and about 1.7x on its little cluster.
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda.dts
@@ -751,6 +751,35 @@
 	pinctrl-names = "default";
 	pinctrl-0 = <&i2c6_pins_a>;
 	clock-frequency = <400000>;
+	/*
+	 * This bus is push-pull on this board, and without saying so every
+	 * register on the DA9214 reads back 0x00.
+	 *
+	 * The symptom is worth describing because it looks exactly like an
+	 * absent or dead chip and was read as one: i2cdetect finds 0x68-0x6b
+	 * (the DA9214's four addresses) and every one of them ACKs, but data
+	 * reads return 0x00, or intermittently fail outright with ENXIO. That
+	 * is what open-drain on a bus with no pull-ups does — the slave can
+	 * still pull the line low to ACK, so addressing works, while the data
+	 * phase never gets a clean high and every bit reads back as zero.
+	 *
+	 * It is also what made B-43 conclude "there is NO DA9214 on this
+	 * board", from mainline da9211 reading DEVICE_ID as 0x0. Stock Android
+	 * on boot1 has the part bound to its own `da9214` driver on this exact
+	 * bus and address, and its device tree carries
+	 * `mediatek,use-push-pull` on i2c@1100e000. We did not.
+	 *
+	 * Mainline i2c-mt65xx honours the property (it writes
+	 * I2C_IO_CONFIG_PUSH_PULL to OFFSET_IO_CONFIG at bus init), so this is
+	 * a one-property fix, and it is safe here: i2c6 has exactly one slave
+	 * and one master.
+	 *
+	 * The vendor additionally drives this part in high-speed mode at
+	 * 3.4 MHz (`I2C_HS_FLAG`, `timing = 3400` in
+	 * drivers/misc/mediatek/power/mt6797/da9214.c). 400 kHz is kept until
+	 * there is a reason to need more; register access does not.
+	 */
+	mediatek,use-push-pull;
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
